== P-7, THE REVOKE. PARTLY PROVED, AND THE UNPROVED HALF IS NAMED.
== 22 September 2026, live project uccixoonmbhrnyczyigt

-- THE HTTP HALF DID NOT RUN, AND NOTHING IS CLAIMED FOR IT.

Run against https://uccixoonmbhrnyczyigt.supabase.co with the project's real
anon key, from this sandbox:

  -- escrow_fund_from_wallet
     HTTP 000
     curl: (56) CONNECT tunnel failed, response 403
  ... the same for escrow_confirm, escrow_request_release,
      escrow_raise_dispute, escrow_hold, escrow_cancel_as and
      escrow_fund_from_wallet_as.

  -- GET /rest/v1/listings -> HTTP 000
     FAIL: the API did not answer at all, so nothing above is evidence.

  == RESULT: FAIL. 8 of 8 checks failed.

THE CONTROL IS THE POINT. Seven refusals in a row look exactly like seven
closed doors, and a probe that counted them would have reported P-7 as the
strongest pass in the file. It reported FAIL, because the eighth check asks an
endpoint that MUST answer and it did not. The egress proxy in this sandbox
denies *.supabase.co by policy:

  curl -sS "$HTTPS_PROXY/__agentproxy/status"
  ... "kind": "connect_rejected",
      "detail": "gateway answered 403 to CONNECT (policy denial or upstream failure)"

So the sentence "an anon key and an authenticated user key call each verb and
each receives a permission error" IS UNPROVEN FROM HERE. It needs one run from
a machine that can reach the project. The script is ready and takes two
environment variables.

-- WHAT IS PROVED, and it is the grant itself.

Read back from the live catalogue after every escrow migration, and asserted
inside each of those migrations so a future one cannot quietly reopen a door:

  verb                                                                anon  authenticated  service_role
  escrow_fund_from_wallet(uuid,uuid,escrow_purpose,bigint,text,int)   f     f              t
  escrow_fund_from_wallet_as(...)                                     f     f              t
  escrow_confirm(uuid)                                                f     f              t
  escrow_confirm_as(uuid,uuid)                                        f     f              t
  escrow_request_release(uuid)                                        f     f              t
  escrow_request_release_as(uuid,uuid)                                f     f              t
  escrow_raise_dispute(uuid,text)                                     f     f              t
  escrow_raise_dispute_as(uuid,uuid,text)                             f     f              t
  escrow_hold(uuid,uuid,bigint,text,text,integer)                     f     f              t
  escrow_open(uuid,uuid,uuid,escrow_purpose,bigint)                   f     f              t
  escrow_release(uuid,uuid,text,text)                                 f     f              t
  escrow_refund(uuid,uuid,text,text)                                  f     f              t
  escrow_cancel_as(uuid,uuid,text)                                    f     f              t
  escrow_admin_resolve(uuid,text,text)                                f     T              t

Every one carries an explicit ACL of {postgres=X/postgres,service_role=X/postgres}
rather than a null ACL, so none of them is relying on the absence of a grant.

escrow_admin_resolve KEEPS its grant to `authenticated` and that is correct,
not an oversight. An admin is an authenticated user and the function guards
itself on the role at its own boundary. The research file records it as a
separate finding from F-2 for exactly this reason.

-- AND THE FIVE-ARGUMENT escrow_hold IS GONE.

F-6 added `hold_days`. Had the old five-argument overload survived, it would
have been a second door with its own grant and its own body that ignores the
hold window. The catalogue shows exactly one `escrow_hold`, with six
arguments. Dropped by its exact old signature in 20260922221000.

-- THE HONEST SUMMARY OF P-7.

The GRANT is proved, twice, from the catalogue and inside the migrations.
The HTTP REFUSAL is UNPROVEN from this sandbox and is the one thing on the
nine-probe list that has not been watched happening.
